    </div>

    <div class="stage-list">
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Spore release</h3>
          <p>Unlike gilled mushrooms, Lion's Mane produces spores on the surface of its hanging spines rather than inside gills. A mature fruiting body sheds a fine white dusting of spores that drift on air currents in search of a wound or crack in hardwood.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Germination</h3>
          <p>A spore that lands in a favorable spot — typically the exposed, damaged wood of a hardwood tree — germinates and sends out a hypha, a single fine thread. It needs to meet and fuse with a compatible hypha from another spore before it can grow further.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Mycelium</h3>
          <p>Once two compatible hyphae fuse, the resulting mycelium spreads through the heartwood, breaking down the wood's structure and absorbing nutrients. This stage can take considerably longer for Lion's Mane than for faster-colonizing species like oysters.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Primordium</h3>
          <p>Once the mycelium is well established and conditions shift — cooler temperatures, higher humidity, a burst of fresh air — it forms a single small knot of tissue at the wound site. Unlike clustering species, Lion's Mane typically produces one primordium at a time rather than many.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Fruiting body</h3>
          <p>The primordium swells into the recognizable white, shaggy mass, growing spines as it matures. Good humidity and airflow keep the spines long and the color bright white; the mushroom is usually harvested just before the spines begin to yellow.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="num"></div>
        <div>
          <h3>Sporulation</h3>
          <p>As the fruiting body fully matures, its spines release a new generation of spores into the air, and the cycle begins again. Left unharvested, a mature Lion's Mane will visibly develop a fine white spore dust on and beneath it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

